<%# One funding rule. Local: `rule` (BudgetPagePresenter::Rule).

    The date is printed only where the rule has one. An anchorless rate rule is never "due" —
    BudgetCalculator#due_date answers the end of the period for one, which is a real number for
    the maths and a lie on screen — so #due_on is nil there and the row stays quiet about dates.

    THE REASON CLAUSE IS GONE WITH THE ORPHANS (two-ledger spec §5). It said why a rule was outside
    the fill order; every rule this page renders is owned by a category, and every category is in
    the waterfall. %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-rule="<%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>">
  <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">
      <%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>
    </span>
    <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 flex items-baseline gap-2">
      <span><%= budget_rule_amount(rule.budget) %></span>
      <% if rule.anchored? %>
        <span class="text-gray-500">· next <%= rule.due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
      <% end %>
      <%= link_to "Edit", edit_budget_path(rule.budget), class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline" %>
    </span>
  </div>
</div>
